10|0@Terraria^1440193417#cub5hom:SpideyScott$:'Eventually' means that you *will* do it one day. That's an assurance. It's not stated as a promise, but it is when you think about it.
-9|0@Terraria^1440194499#cub64nj:BCProgramming$:If I say "I will eventually show up at the party" to somebody, I did not promise to show up at the party. I stated my *intent* to show up at the party. I did not assure them that I would be at the party. If anybody says later "you promised you would be at the party" *they are wrong* because I didn't promise anything- I stated my intention. Even if I said "I will be at the party" that is still not a promise. Statements of Intent are not assurance on their own. A promise requires assurance, and assurance requires more than statement of intent. One particular example: &gt;Once sales start dying and a minimum time has passed, I will release the game source code as some kind of open source. This was a statement of *intent* from Notch. As it turns out- Shit happens and things don't turn out the way we expected. One of the problems with independent development is that by being more open to the community, the community can easily interpret what you say however they desire. In this case people turn off-the-cuff remarks into "promises" and "call out" the developer when they don't keep them. 
